The Gherkin representation is chosen for its readability and alignment with user-facing scenarios, which is beneficial for capturing the behavioral aspects of the requirements. It is also simple and clear, making it easy for an LLM to generate valid tests. On the other hand, xUnit test cases are selected for their ability to perform concrete, executable tests that can directly validate the functional aspects of the code, ensifying the requirements are met at the implementation level. Both representations offer a balance between abstraction and specificity, and they complement each other in testing the requirements from both a user interaction perspective and a code implementation perspective.
